All five headers: `# tree: dirty:0994a3b25bc740f34ccf6a48c4e17dcc70a647e9`.
Recomputed in §1: that is the source tree of 215987e, the spec-gate commit,
in which W-069 is merged. Tree-hash ancestry PASSES on all five.

Disclosed method: the builder implemented all five behaviours in one
continuous working-tree pass, verified them green, then stubbed each
implementation piece to a no-op, recorded the five reds against that stubbed
state, and restored byte-for-byte from backups. The 46-second span is
consistent with ONE stubbed state that all five reds were taken against,
not five stub/restore cycles.

### 5.2 Does the brief authorize it? No. Verbatim, its red section:

  "**Reds are assertion-level only if the module loads**, so the builder
   lands skeletons first — `tick.ts`'s `DueProbe`/`ProbeInputs` types and the
   `maxTurns` field — then records each behaviour's red from its own
   single-behaviour run."

  "**Tree-hash ancestry.** Reds are recorded on this opus's work branch,
   against a tree in which **W-069 is already merged** and the skeletons
   above are landed and empty."

Two clauses: a prescribed ORDER (skeletons, then reds, then implementation)
and a prescribed STATE (the three named skeletons landed, everything else
empty). Neither is advisory; they are this opus's own answer to the studio's
load-bearing standing rule.

### 5.3 The artifacts themselves prove the tree was not the prescribed one

reds/W-071/01.log:
    PASS  b1 runTick gathers through opts.listModels
    PASS  b1 runTick gathers through opts.versions

Against "the skeletons above landed and empty" — `DueProbe`/`ProbeInputs`
types plus the `maxTurns` field, nothing more — `runTick` contains no
`opts.listModels ?? codexListModels` call at all, so those two assertions
CANNOT pass. They passed. The tree these reds were taken against was a
partially-stubbed implementation, not the brief's empty skeleton. This is
internal to the evidence and needs no disclosure to establish.

### 5.4 Do the bodies demonstrate more than the prescribed order, or less? LESS.

Three measured places:

(i)  01.log — the two assertions above lost their red outright. Skeleton-first
     records them as FAIL; this method records them as PASS.
(ii) 03.log — `PASS  b3 w071: positive control — no cap yields 5 start calls
     and an empty skipped` is green BECAUSE NO CAP EXISTED. The positive
     control's stated purpose is to prove the assertion cannot pass by the
     feature being absent; it passed while the feature was absent. (The
     prescribed skeleton produces the same defect, so this one is a hole in
     the brief's behaviour-3 design, not in the order — but it points the
     same way.)
(iii)03.log — `PASS  b3 w071: the 3 skipped pairs are unverified on disk,
     never re-spent` is green with its own evidence column reading
     [["p1","available"],…,["p5","available"]]: the assertion passed while
     its subject was false, because `skippedIds` was empty and `[].every()`
     is true.

Plus the vacuous-by-emptiness passes the method shares with skeleton-first:
02.log's "(e) run two probes exactly the 3 stranding would have lost" (green
on `[]`), "after a complete battery, a second computeDue yields no probe item"
(green because no probe item is ever emitted), 04.log's L0 and paused
zero-battery-call assertions. Those are why the brief demanded positive
controls, and 04.log's positive controls did correctly fail.

Net: the method produced strictly weaker reds than the order the spec
prescribed. It is not a lateral substitution.

### 5.5 Is the class distinguishable from backfill by anything IN the artifacts? No.

`# tree: dirty:<hash>` names the COMMITTED HEAD source tree, never the working
tree's content (verified: 0994a3b2 is exactly 215987e's source tree, computed
with the working tree in an unknown state). Skeleton-first and
implement-verify-stub-record-restore therefore emit byte-identical headers,
byte-identical behaviour numbers, byte-identical exit codes. The ONLY reason
this review knows which occurred is that the builder said so.

By this censor's own W-067 ruling — verifiability by design, not by luck — a
gate whose compliance is knowable only through the candour of the party being
gated is not verified. And the W-065 05.log ruling ("a red manufactured by
reverting working code is backfilled evidence, forbidden") that no censor can
ever apply from the artifacts is dead letter; accepting it here repeals it by
acquiescence.

### 5.6 Against the precedent line

(a) W-047's mutation-record reds — AUTHORIZED because THE SPEC designed them
    that way for already-correct code. Not on point: this spec designed the
    opposite, in the two clauses quoted in 5.2. A builder cannot unilaterally
    substitute an evidence method its own spec forecloses; naming the method
    is the entire reason a spec names it.
(b) W-046 r3's stash — ACCEPTED because the recorded tree WAS the true
    pre-fix state. The distinguishing property is absent here: the recorded
    tree was a hand-crafted stub of a tree that had already been fully
    implemented and verified green. §5.3 proves it was not even the stub the
    spec described.
(c) W-065's 05.log ruling — FORBIDDEN. This is that mechanism exactly:
    implement -> verify green -> revert to no-op -> record red -> restore.
    The standing rule "Evidence is produced, never backfilled" regulates the
    ACT, not the artifact. A red's whole epistemic content is "at the moment
    the implementation did not exist, this assertion failed"; recorded after
    the implementation existed and was hidden, it proves only "removing this
    code fails the test" — a mutation result, which is a different and
    weaker claim, and one that needs a spec's authorization (see (a)).
(d) W-069's skeleton-first — AUTHORIZED, and what this brief prescribes.

The case produces artifacts indistinguishable from (d) with the time order of
(c). (c) controls, for the four reasons above: the spec forbids it, the
artifacts prove the tree was wrong, the bodies are weaker not stronger, and
nothing in the evidence could ever have caught it.

### 5.7 For the standing red-ordering petitio

Load-bearing, and the reason this section matters beyond one opus: THESE FIVE
REDS PASS TREE-ANCESTRY. Every one names dirty:<spec-gate source tree>, with
W-069 merged, behaviours 1..5 contiguous, exit 1, non-empty bodies, inside the
spec-gate-to-implementation window. A rule built on tree ancestry — which is
what the petitio proposes — cannot catch this class AT ALL, and would have
certified all five.

What would catch it: `bisellium red` recording an identity of the WORKING TREE
alongside the commit tree — a content hash (or the diff itself) over the
opus's declared files at the moment the red ran. Then a red taken against an
empty skeleton is distinguishable from a red taken against a stubbed
implementation, and from a red taken against restored-then-reverted code,
without relying on the builder's account. That is the concrete amendment the
petitio needs; tree ancestry alone is the wrong hash for this job for the
same reason `git rev-parse HEAD^{tree}` is the wrong hash for a certificate.

