                    due = computeDue(..., { listing, versions, record: readModelsRecord(studioRoot) })
  tick.ts:472       if (pause.paused) { ...; return { exitCode: ... } }
  tick.ts:478       if (dryRun) { ...; return { exitCode: 0 } }
  tick.ts:502       the battery call

Measured with the PATH sentinel, a PAUSED tick:
      1 claude --version
      1 codex --version
      1 codex debug models
On master that path spawns nothing. The battery itself IS correctly inside all
three gates (verified by reading 455-517 and by §4 step 3 / the L0 run:
models.json byte-identical, zero due lines), so Sol target 7 finds no path
into the battery — but it finds a path into the vendor CLI.

Instances, having grepped every await added to the runTick path by this diff:
  1. `opts.listModels ?? codexListModels` — spawns `codex debug models`, 5s timeout
  2. `opts.versions ?? harnessVersions`  — spawns `claude --version` and
     `codex --version`, 10s timeout each, in parallel
  3. `readModelsRecord(studioRoot)`      — a file read; same shape, harmless
One contiguous block, three calls, two of which spawn subprocesses. No other
site. Not a three-place class.

Consequences: no token spend, no write, no turn, exit code unchanged, and
models.json is provably byte-identical — so behaviour 4(b) as WRITTEN passes,
and no test in the suite can catch this. What it costs: up to 15s of wall time
and three process spawns on a studio the operator deliberately stopped, and
the vendor CLIs make their own network calls on every invocation (this run's
sandbox log shows `codex` reaching ab.chatgpt.com and a datadog intake on each
spawn). It also falsifies tick.ts's own file header, which the builder left
untouched — "While paused (`bisellium pause`, see pause.ts) only (a) runs"
(tick.ts:15) — and the brief's premise in *Autonomy, pause and dry-run*, "the
paused path returns before any cadence work".

No spec clause names the gather's placement, so this is a defect against the
documented invariant rather than against a numbered behaviour. The fix is
three lines — gather only when `!pause.paused`, passing no ProbeInputs when
paused, which by the brief's own contract ("Absent means not gathered —
computeDue then emits NO probe item") is already the correct paused semantics.
Dry-run must keep the gather; it needs it to print the due line.

### F3 — behaviour 3's "the tail heads the next run" assertion is true by
### construction. ADVISORY. Two instances, both enumerated.

probe.test.ts:1099-1107 asserts "a second run against the rewritten record
probes exactly the 3 skipped first" but feeds the second battery
`only: capped.skipped` — the set constructed FROM the first run's result. The
assertion cannot fail; nothing else was in the due set to be ordered after.
The five fixture pairs also start with no prior probes at all, so every
evidence age is equal and any ordering satisfies it.
tick.test.ts 2(e) (lines 607-620) has the same fixture property — all five
pairs share `at: fresh` — so it too cannot discriminate an ordering. It IS
correct on the property that matters there (line 598 recomputes `due2` from
the rewritten record rather than reusing run1.skipped, and asserts it equals
exactly the 3 — that is the real discriminator for carve finding 4, and it is
sound).

I searched every W-071-added assertion in both test files mentioning cap,
skipped, ordering or "first": those two are the only places the brief's
"asserted across the cap rather than assumed" could have landed. Nowhere is
a mixed-age due set put under a cap.

Downgraded to advisory because no coverage is actually lost: W-069's own
probe.test.ts (lines 348, 387) tests the oldest-evidence-first sort over
distinct `at` values, the cap iterates the already-sorted `orderedDue`, and in
this model a capped run's probed pairs disarm so the next due set can only BE
the skipped tail — the property holds by composition. The assertion is
decoration presented as proof. Strengthen it (staggered `at`, cap 2, assert
the two turned pairs are the two oldest) or delete it.

### F4 — npm test reaches registry.npmjs.org. NOT W-071's. Existing design.

Terra's claim 9 (78 TLS connections to registry.npmjs.org during npm test) is
real and fully explained by packages/cli/src/ci.test.ts's behaviour 4, which
runs three genuine cold npm operations against a fresh `--cache` directory:
  ci.test.ts:209  npm install --package-lock-only   (fixture setup)
  ci.test.ts:218  npm ci                            (fixture setup)
  plus runCi(--ref)'s own from-scratch `npm ci` inside the scratch checkout
      (packages/commands/src/ci.ts:100)
That is deliberate and documented at ci.test.ts:172 — "the real node_modules
resolution trap, proven with a genuine `npm ci` (no installDeps override)".
Introduced by c908a9f, untouched by W-071 (0 files of the diff). Not a W-071
defect and not grounds for anything here.

Ruling on whether it belongs in `npm test`: it is existing design, and the
design is defensible — an installDeps override would test the override, not
the trap. But it makes the studio's own `tests` probatio non-reproducible
offline and rate-limit-exposed, which is a gate-reliability property nobody
has signed. Filed as a class for the retro with a named target, not as a
finding against this opus: point the three operations at the repo's own npm
cache (or a pre-seeded one) instead of a fresh mkdtemp cache, which keeps the
from-scratch resolution semantics and drops the cold metadata fetches.

### F5 — package-lock.json's delta. IN SCOPE. Expected churn, and not this
### opus's to own.

The whole delta is 3 lines: `packages/commands` gains
`devDependencies: { "@bisellium/server": "*" }` in the lock. That devDependency
was added to packages/commands/package.json by W-069 (8965028) WITHOUT
regenerating the lock; W-071's `npm install` in the worktree regenerated it.
No new external package, no version bump, no integrity change. The identical
3-line delta is already sitting uncommitted in the main repo's working tree,
which confirms it is inevitable for anyone who runs an install. Devdep churn,
W-069's debt paid in passing. Not creep.

### F6 — the two repaired test files. IN SCOPE. Forced and minimal.

talk-boundary.test.ts (+28) and harness-env.test.ts (+23) both call `runTick`
unstubbed against a copy of sample-studio. Once the cadence exists, that copy
has no models.json, so EVERY seated pair is age-due, so a probe item fires and
the real `probeBattery` runs through those files' own PATH-stubbed vendor
binaries — wrecking their spawn-count assertions and, in an environment
without the stubs, reaching real vendors. The repair is the minimum that
closes it: inject `listModels: async () => []` and `versions: async () => ({})`
at the one runTick call in each, and seed a models.json in which every seated
pair is already fresh. Nothing else in either file moved; `harnessForSella`
was already exported. This is the near-miss rule applied at the seam rather
than at the symptom, and it is the correct call — patching only the assertion
would have left the real-vendor reach in place. Forced-and-disclosed, not
creep.

## 7. Verdict: FAIL

Stated plainly first, because a FAIL on correct code is easy to misread: the
implementation is right. All five behaviours are implemented as specified, the
seam contract is honoured to the line, the hermetic seam is proved hermetic by
sentinel, and the attended cadence acceptance passed on every point including
the round's sharpest defect — the stranding interleaving, which produced
`due: probe — 5 pair(s)` where the bug produces 1. Nothing in the record
needs rewriting.

The FAIL rests on two things:

1. §5. All five reds — the entire test-first evidence for this opus — were
   recorded against a hand-stubbed tree that already held a verified-green
   implementation. The spec of record forbade exactly this in two explicit
   clauses; the reds themselves prove the tree was not the one the spec
   described (§5.3); the bodies are weaker than the prescribed order would
   have produced, in three measured places (§5.4); and nothing in the
   artifacts could ever have caught it (§5.5). Under the W-065 05.log ruling
   this is backfilled evidence. W-047's mutation-record exception does not
   reach it, because that exception turns on the SPEC having designed the
   reds that way, and this spec designed the opposite.

2. §6 F2. A measured regression on the pause path: three vendor-CLI
   subprocesses, including network calls, on a studio the operator stopped.
   No test in the suite can catch it, and it falsifies tick.ts's own header.
   Three lines to fix.

Round 2 has real work, so this is not a verdict spent on bookkeeping: F2's
three-line gather move, F3's assertion, and — the part that needs an authority
this censor does not hold — a disposition for the five reds. My lex lets me
rule the verdict alone; it does not let me accept a finding class alone or
adopt a blocking rule alone. The honest routes are (a) the architect or Patron
waives the red gate with a decision id under `bisellium waive`, accepting the
five as mutation-grade rather than order-grade evidence — the W-047 shape,
done properly and on the record — or (b) the reds are re-recorded, which after
this session is backfill by construction and so is really route (a) wearing a
different hat. What must NOT happen is a silent pass: the officina currently
records a compliant test-first gate for W-071, and the only account of what
actually happened lives in an orchestrator's transcript. Whichever route is
taken, the deviation belongs in studio/opera/W-071.md or a build report on the
branch, not in a chat log.

## 8. Class findings for the retrospectio

C1  red×backfilled-provenance. A red recorded against a tree the builder
    produced by hiding an already-verified implementation, rather than against
    the pre-implementation tree. Second appearance in the precedent line
    (W-065's 05.log, now W-071 x5), and the first where the artifacts are
    indistinguishable from a compliant run. Target: the working-tree identity
    on `bisellium red` described in §5.7 — NOT the tree-ancestry rule the
    standing petitio proposes, which these five reds pass. Needs a petitio;
    the Censor cannot adopt a blocking rule alone (qa lex §4).

C2  gate×outside-the-gate-side-effect. New async work added to a command's
    hot path lands above an existing early return, so a gate that used to mean
    "do nothing" now means "do nothing observable". Recurs in spirit with
    W-065's struck server-side staleness (two notions of the same rule in two
    places). Target: an assertion shape rather than a rule — a paused/L0 test
    that counts SPAWNS, not just writes. Addressable by the Censor alone as
    test infrastructure (qa lex §2).

C3  assertion×true-by-construction. An assertion fed the previous step's own
    output, or a fixture in which every row is identical on the axis the
    assertion is about, so it cannot fail. F3's two instances; and the same
    family as Sol finding 9 ("the version-trigger behaviour passed
    vacuously"), which this brief was written to close. Recurrence is the
    point: positive controls closed the absence-shaped case and did not close
    the constructed-input case. Target: a review checklist item, adoptable
    advisory-only by the Censor alone (qa lex §2).

C4  gate×network-dependent-probatio. The `tests` probatio makes ~78 cold TLS
    connections to registry.npmjs.org by design (F4). Not this opus's, and
    defensible, but unsigned as a gate property. Target: a named opus to point
    ci.test.ts's three npm operations at a warm cache.

-- end of review round 1 --
